<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Dabuyid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">642 CE – 760 CE (approx.) · capital Sari</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last Zoroastrian rulers of Tabaristan, who kept a Sasanian-style principality alive on the Caspian coast for over a century after the Arab conquest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>According to later tradition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gil-gavbara">Gil Gavbara</a>, a prince from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> claiming Sasanian descent, took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> around 642, as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> collapsed under Arab attack. His descendants, named after his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dabuya">Dabuya</a>, became the ispahbads of Tabaristan, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sari-iran">Sari</a> and the coastal lowlands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Dabuyids kept Sasanian forms of rule and Zoroastrian religion and minted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a> script on the Sasanian model, still dated by their own era. Protected by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alborz">Alborz</a> and dense forests, they resisted repeated Umayyad attacks. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhan-the-great">Farrukhan the Great</a> defeated the Arab general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazid-ibn-al-muhallab">Yazid ibn al-Muhallab</a> in 717, and the dynasty paid tribute only when forced.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last ispahbad, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khurshid-of-tabaristan">Khurshid</a>, rebelled against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, and in 759 to 760 Abbasid armies overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>. Khurshid took poison, and Tabaristan became a caliphal province, though the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bavand-dynasty">Bavand dynasty</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/qarinvand-dynasty">Qarinvand dynasty</a> survived in the mountains.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dabuyid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Dabuyid dynasty" width="320" height="170"><figcaption>Image: Gabagool and edited by HistoryofIran (me) · CC BY 1.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DabuyidDynastyGreatestExtent.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>642 CE – 760 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sari</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Farrukhan the Great, Dadhburzmihr, Khurshid</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zoroastrianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Persian, Tabari</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dabuyids, Gil-Gavbarids</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=701&amp;focus=dabuyid-dynasty" aria-label="Dabuyid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6013 859 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dabuyid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6333 1115L6357 1235M6333 1115L6134 1224"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6357 1235h0M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6333 1115h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6333 1115h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sari. <a href="/atlas?year=701&amp;focus=dabuyid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
